I made a mistake: I used a raw string `r'...\u590d...'` which treats `\u590d` literally as backslash-u-590d, not as a unicode escape. In Python, raw strings don't process unicode escapes. I need to use a normal string with `\u590d` OR use the actual characters via a different method.

Let me use normal (non-raw) strings with `\u` escapes: `'C:\\Users\\Administrator\\Desktop\\wiki\\agent\u590d\u76d8'`. In a normal string, `\u590d` is processed as unicode escape.

But wait — there's the PowerShell here-string issue. When I write `\u590d` in the here-string, it's just ASCII characters backslash-u-590d, which get passed to Python. Python then interprets `\u590d` as a unicode escape (in a normal string literal). That should work.

